WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Steve: We have Mayu here, Mayu E Room.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.160
Steve: And today we're going to 
talk about English language,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.750
Steve: education or language education in general

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.650
Steve: with specific reference to Japan,
but not only.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.520
Steve: And I leave a link to Mayu’s YouTube channel

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.570
Steve: in the description box.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:18.270
Steve: Hi Mayu.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.930
Mayu: Hi! Thanks for having me.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.230
Steve: OK, I’ve been looking forward 
because you know,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.130
Steve: I lived in Japan for nine years 
at the country that I, I really enjoy.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:30.650
Steve: I love being in Japan.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.780
Steve: So many interesting things happen there,

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.180
Steve: And one of the things that 
always comes up is that,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.500
Steve: you know, Japan,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.820
Steve: They have more people taking 
TOEIC than any other country

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.260
Steve: and they don't do very well.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.730
Steve: And I like to talk to you about this

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.300
Steve: because you speak such excellent English.
Mayu: Thank you.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.520
Steve: Not only have learned English, 
and I gather,

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.440
Steve: you're learning Spanish, 

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.110
Steve: and you also study at the 
Middlebury College in the United States

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.340
Steve: language acquisitions. So…

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.180
Steve: Tell me. Why is it you think that

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:07.940
Steve: Japanese people seem to have more
 difficulty with English than…

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.340
Steve: I mean,

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:13.440
Steve: Geographically I can understand that too, 
but what are some other problems?

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:18.180
Steve: Let’s put it this way. What are some other problems with English language education in Japan.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:27.360
Mayu: I think we're actually not so bad
 at English like on paper.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.970
Mayu: As in like, We are good at reading 

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.760
Mayu: and writing I think relatively

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.080
Mayu: but for listening and speaking is 
very difficult for us

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.270
Mayu: because we have really 
zero opportunity in Japan

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.720
Mayu: to listen to real English.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.040
Mayu: And we have like…

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.440
Mayu: We don't have any reason to speak English 
outside of the classrooms

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.490
Mayu: in the only, like reason we study English is
Steve: Absolutely.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.750
Mayu: because we need that 
knowledge for the exams to…

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.070
Mayu: you know, enter University.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.120
Mayu: And also

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.900
Mayu: I think we are told like 
there is right and wrong in a lot of things 

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:16.190
Mayu: So like…

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:21.290
Mayu: If a pronunciation or grammar 
isn’t good enough 

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.610
Mayu: We are not really like…
 people are going to be judgmental.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.770
Mayu: That's what we think like deep down,
Steve: Right.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.370
Mayu: And that's not just language education 

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:36.970
Mayu: That's like instilled in our education like, 
even in the, the family, I think

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:42.110
Steve: Okay. I should say by the way that we are going to speak half in English and half in Japanese.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.300
Mayu: But I wanted to have at least half in English 

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.410
Steve: so people can hear your excellent English.
Mayu: Thank you.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.620
Steve: And obviously It's true.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.610
Steve: I mean if you live in Japan,

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.460
Steve: it's 130 million people

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:55.130
Steve: and that you don't really need 
English for most situations

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.240
Steve: and yet at some level in Japan,

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.520
Steve: I mean if I go to a bookstore in Japan 
I go to the language section,

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.760
Steve: all the books, so many books about learning English 
so many book about TOEIC.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.600
Steve: There is, there is a lot of interested.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.830
Mayu: And there's a lot of like, 
online platforms that you can learn English.

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:21.270
Mayu: Now that’s like… after, I think after 
COVID started it has expanded a lot

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.010
Mayu: because people had 
so much more time at home,

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.600
Mayu: and they wanted to start something new, 

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.520
Mayu: they maybe wanted to 
prepare themselves to study or work abroad.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.820
Mayu: So there is a lot of resources,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.780
Mayu: yet, we still struggle to learn 
acquire the language.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.930
Steve: Now, you went to Middlebury College.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.310
Steve: So, first of all that would be,

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.530
Steve: you know, obviously
 I have the same experience

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.240
Steve: when they went to live in France 
as a nineteen-year-old

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.660
Steve: when we as a student, go to another country, 
a different culture, different language.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.550
Steve: It's a wonderful experience,

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.170
Steve: but you started language acquisition,
I think among other thing.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.940
Steve: And what did you learn in Middlebury College,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.900
Steve: that was different from 
how languages are taught in Japan.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:12.930
Mayu: OK, I think, one of most interesting things was
 I took Spanish at Middlebury.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.030
Mayu: And I also took Spanish in Japan

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:18.540
Mayu: and there was a huge difference.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.970
Mayu: In Japan,
it was more focused on.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:26.510
Mayu: Like, how much vocabulary do you know.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:27.580
Mayu: How…

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.940
Mayu: like a lot of like, you know, these like…

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:36.410
Mayu: [ Speaking Spanish ]

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:37.640
Mayu: most of a lot of that

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.640
Mayu: In Japan,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:45.310
Steve: Conjugations
Mayu: Yes! Conjugations but in middlebury 

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:51.200
Mayu: we were forced to speak 
the language in every single class

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.060
Mayu: and we also had to read a lot,
 like it was…

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:02.850
Mayu: It was… above My Level of the book

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:05.460
Mayu: the books were like…
I couldn’t read them

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.610
Mayu: but we had to, so difficult because I had…
Steve: That’s good.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.890
Mayu: I had my dictionary next to me 
all the time when I was like

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:18.620
Mayu: OK, I don’t know this one this one like
I don’t know anything 

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:23.230
Mayu: So I was like… much harder than my level 

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:24.980
Mayu: So…
Steve: But you know,

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.110
Mayu: And we also had to like present in Spanish

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.000
Mayu: like we have to make a 
presentation in Spanish

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.540
Mayu: that wasn't anything like 
what I learned in Japan.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.310
Steve: Right, but you know,

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.210
Steve: one thing you said the 
Japanese people can all read well

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.520
Steve: but how common is it is

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:50.270
Steve: it in Japan for people to read
 novels in English, a full novel.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.000
Mayu: Why do we not do that? 
Steve: No no, I’m saying,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:54.930
Steve: Do people do that?

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.230
Steve: Is that common that 
people would read novels in English?

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.550
Mayu: No, I think…

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:06.160
Mayu: When I discuss some of things
with my friends from America

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.410
Mayu: He says… like why,

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:12.610
Mayu: He asks me like, 
why do you not read books?

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:16.550
Mayu: but you learned like, 
like even in Japanese.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.220
Mayu: We don't really read like the whole books.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:22.880
Mayu: Like that's like a ancient books like…

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:28.160
Mayu: Classical Japanese literature  
or something and in the US.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.020
Mayu: When I also studied in high school in the US,

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.610
Mayu: we had to read a lot of literature,
like you know,

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.600
Mayu: a lot of The Great Gatsby,
 Catcher in the Rye or something,

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.840
Mayu: and I never had that experience in Japan.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:46.470
Mayu: and we would read like 
an excerpt from the books,

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.060
Mayu: but not the whole thing.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.490
Mayu: So we would just like read 
the summary of the books like…

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:55.390
Mayu: pretend that we understand.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:58.950
Mayu: So we don’t really do that in Japanese.
Steve: So I'm a great believer in reading books.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.130
Steve: I'm a great believer in reading books.

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:01.740
Every language

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.430
Steve: I've learned it when I can 
read a book from cover to cover,

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:07.290
Steve: that's a major achievement,

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:12.710
Steve: I often hear people say “Oh, I can read as well. 
What do you read? I read newspaper articles, read a book.”

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.690
you know, people who read well

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:16.800
Steve: and that's part of what's in laying

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:17.480
Steve: because with laying 

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.700
Steve: You can actually… You don’t have to 
keep on looking words up in the dictionary,

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.060
Steve: You can actually look them up and they…

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:24.630
Steve: developed, you know,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.960
Steve: List of words and how many words 
 you've learned something

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.800
Steve: That the whole function of,
I think reading is underestimated.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.300
Steve: And I'm not just reading short articles 
but actually reading books

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:39.900
Steve: I think it’s very important in terms of getting 
the language into your brain.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.060
Steve: Japan…

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:49.130
Steve: But of course there are people who learn English 
to a very high level of proficiency in Japan

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:51.260
Steve: it’s not everyone, but…

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:56.160
Steve: I think the Japanese tend to think of 
themselves as being poor Learners of English.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.500
Steve: We are doing poorer than 
the Koreans or the Chinese.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:02.700
Steve: Other people in Asia are better than we are,

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:05.660
Steve: I'm not sure that that's the case.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:09.760
Steve: So, Let’s speak in Japanese.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:20.660
Steve: Basically, For Japanese people, 
English is just a different kind of language.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:27.330
Mayu: Yes.
Steve: Japanese is such a specific language.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:36.330
Steve: So many words are from China
but it’s not like Chinese.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:44.510
Steve: Japanese is really… Unique.
Mayu: Unique.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:52.190
Steve: But these vocabularies,
there’s a common vocabularies like Chinese and Korean.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:59.700
Steve: But there’s no common vocabularies 
on the language in Europe.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:02.700
Steve: That’s what makes it more difficult.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:09.440
Steve: But I could say the same thing 
when foreigners study Japanese.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.840
Steve: In my opinion,

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:16.860
Steve: rather than difficulty and 
difference of language, 

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.040
Steve: it depends on the attitude of 
the person who studies and…

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:25.790
Steve: how much you use those language, 
These are more important.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:30.900
Steve: It’s not about 
how objectively difficult Japanese is.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.960
Steve: It’s not about that.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:37.150
Steve: It’s about wanting to study. Among those reasons 
like it because Japan is a interesting country

00:09:37.150 --> 00:09:43.420
Steve: or Japanese movies are nice
and doing lots of reading and listening every day,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:53.920
Steve: I think that’s the important factor 
rather than level of difficulty of language.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:57.790
Steve: What do you think?
Mayu: Well…

00:09:57.790 --> 00:09:59.790
Mayu: I think…

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:13.180
Mayu: I think they shouldn’t let students 
read some summary of complicated books.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:15.990
Mayu: They should read more easy stories.
Steve: Right.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:24.590
Mayu: They can start with those books like toddlers
 in the US are reading and find it it’s interesting.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:33.250
Mayu: Or School should adopt stories like Harry Potter
 or Disney that everybody already likes.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:39.700
Mayu: Then I think people would 
take English more willingly.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:44.190
Mayu: Now, These are difficult.
Steve: Yes indeed, I agree with you.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:52.480
Steve: I think Harry Potter did such a 
contribution at education of English.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:58.880
Steve: And as you mentioned earlier, the book 
“Catcher in the Rye” is not so difficult either, Right?

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:01.350
Mayu: Yes, I think it was easy to read.

00:11:01.350 --> 00:11:03.010
Steve: Right.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:09.860
Steve: People should use those type of books,
something that they would enjoy reading.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:20.170
Steve: You should read modern books rather than 
reading books that is hundreds of years ago.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:21.600
Steve: Right.

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:32.800
Steve: But for Japanese people, English is 
a language that they just forced to study.

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:37.280
Steve: I think it’s just like that for most of them.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.710
Steve: They don’t start studying
 because they would love to.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:43.580
Mayu: Yes… Well.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:48.220
Mayu: How they teach at school,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:54.750
Mayu: they start with “ABCD“ song.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:12:00.100
Mayu: But as soon as I enter to middle school,

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:06.300
Mayu: they taught me like “I have a pen.” is 
using “Have” and “Have“ is one of general verb.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:11.650
Mayu: They started like that
 with conceptual way and…

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:16.130
Mayu: I think this is one of the reason why 
people show no desire towards English language.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:17.340
Steve: Right.

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:21.630
Steve: I could say the same thing in Canada.

00:12:21.630 --> 00:12:25.960
Steve: They have to study French at school.
Mayu: Right.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:30.430
Steve: People told to acquire the language 
because it’s just one of common language, 

00:12:30.430 --> 00:12:33.470
Steve: that’s where they loose their interest. 

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:36.350
Steve: In Sweden and Netherlands,

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:40.780
Steve: all of their shows on TV are originals.

00:12:40.780 --> 00:12:45.860
Steve: Let’s say if the show is from England, 
they just put it on in English.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:49.330
Steve: They don’t put dubbed shows.

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:55.390
Steve: Therefore when 5 or 6 or 7 years old 
children start going to school,

00:12:55.390 --> 00:13:00.720
Steve: they are already familiar with English.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.660
Steve: That is such a big factor.

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:08.230
Steve: If the beginning of… I forgot the word…

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:11.060
Mayu: Wing it?
Steve: Yes, Wing it!

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:16.790
Steve: If you start from “I have, You have, He has,” something like that, 

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.680
Steve: of course people would have
 an adverse reaction to it.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:23.870
Steve: That’s why you need to 
start with another way.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:27.280
Mayu: Actually, when I was small.

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:31.860
Mayu: I was acting on theatrical performance in English
and I didn’t understand the meaning

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.550
Mayu: But I was singing something like
 “Weather weather is fine.“ 

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:38.220
Mayu: and I didn’t know what it meant.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:41.380
Mayu: I think about it now and 
I know it was about good weather.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:42.820
Mayu: Back then,

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:47.390
Mayu: I felt like this song is just so fun, 
not like Japanese songs.

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:50.180
Mayu: And then I entered middle school.

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:56.030
Mayu: And I found out that 
weather song was based on these rules.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:14:00.000
Mayu: It was like something extra to
 learn after I enjoyed the song.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.800
Mayu: Therefore it was 
good starting point for me.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:09.150
Mayu: So I would recommend to start with 
something attract people’s interest.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:13.110
Steve: Starting with something fun,
 that is important.

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:18.060
Steve: If you don’t enjoy studying language, 
it would be difficult.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:19.470
Mayu: Yes, you can not continue.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:28.700
Steve: So Today, for people 
who is watching this video.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:46.540
Steve: There’s many advices and tips of studying English 
for Japanese people on Mayu san’s channel.

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:50.760
Steve: So I would leave a link on description box.
Mayu: Thank you.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:15:00.800
Steve: And for foreigners 
who is studying Japanese.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:12.990
Steve: I would put part of discussion in Japanese 
as one of Japanese lesson of LingQ.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:23.620
Steve: People who is studying Japanese can use those and 
hopefully this would help to improve their Japanese.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:30.740
Steve: So Thank you Mayu san for your time.
Mayu: Thank you.

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:33.720
Steve: Thank you.
Mayu: Goodbye.

